WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.509
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:10.250
I would like to show you some results from
the area of 3D printing, a topic which is,

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:15.599
I think, a little overlooked, and show you
that works this field are improving at an

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:17.100
incredible pace.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.130
Now, a common theme among research papers
in this area is that they typically allow

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:27.790
us to design objects and materials by thinking
about how they should look.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:34.269
Let’s see if this is really true by applying
the Second Law of Papers, which says whatever

00:00:34.269 --> 00:00:39.340
you are thinking about, there is already a
Two Minute Papers episode on that.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.800
Let’s see if it applies here!

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.679
For instance, just prescribing a shape for
3D printing is old-old news.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:52.519
Here is a previous technique that is able
to print auxetic materials.

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:58.250
These are materials that we can start stretching,
and if we do, instead of thinning, they get

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.319
fatter.

00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:03.960
We can also 3D print filigree patterns with
ease.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.760
These are detailed, thin patterns typically
found in jewelry, fabrics and ornaments, and

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.620
as you may imagine, crafting such motifs on
objects would be incredibly laborious to do

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.480
by hand.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:23.790
We can also prescribe an image, and 3D print
an object that will cast a caustic pattern

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:29.880
that shows exactly that image.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.700
And printing textured 3D objects in a number
of different ways is also possible.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:43.580
This is called hydrographic printing and is
one of the most flamboyant ways of doing that.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.740
So what happens here?

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:54.130
Well, we place a film in water, use a chemical
activator spray on it, and shove the object

00:01:54.130 --> 00:02:02.280
in the water, and…oh yes, there we go!

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.730
Note that these were all showcased in previous
episodes of this series.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:12.519
So, in 3D printing, we typically design things
by how they should look.

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:15.420
Of course, how else would we be designing?

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.700
Well, the authors of this crazy paper don’t
care about looks at all.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.739
Well, what else would they care about if not
the looks?

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:28.030
Get this, they care about how these objects
deform.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:35.090
Yes, with this work, we can design deformations,
and the algorithm will find out what the orientation

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.170
of the fibers should be to create a prescribed
effect.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.900
Okay, but what does this really mean?

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:48.889
This means that we can now 3D print really
cool, fiber-like microstructures deform well

00:02:48.889 --> 00:02:50.420
from one direction.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:56.239
In other words, they can be smashed easily
and flatten a great deal during that process.

00:02:56.239 --> 00:03:00.430
I bet there was a ton of fun to be had at
the lab on this day.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:08.359
However, research is not only fun and joy,
look, if we turn this object around, ouch.

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:15.030
This side is very rigid, and resists deformations
well, so there was probably a lot of injuries

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.269
in the lab that day too.

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:19.639
So, clearly, this is really cool.

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:24.480
But of course, our question is, what is all
this good for?

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.279
Is this just an interesting experiment, or
is this thing really useful?

00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:34.719
Well, let’s see what this paper has to offer
in 5 amazing examples.

00:03:34.719 --> 00:03:37.459
Example number one.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:38.459
Pliers.

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:44.379
The jaws and the hand grips are supposed to
be very rigid, checkmark, however, there needs

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:48.049
to be a joint between them to allow us to
operate it.

00:03:48.049 --> 00:03:54.620
This joint needs to be deformable, and not
any kind of deformable, but exactly the right

00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:00.260
kind of deformable to make sure it opens and
closes properly.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.430
Loving this one.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.129
3D printing pliers from fiber-like structures.

00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:06.919
How cool is that?

00:04:06.919 --> 00:04:09.549
Example number two.

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.040
Structured plates.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.670
This shows that not all sides have to have
the same properties.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:20.359
We can also print a material which has rigid
and flexible parts on the same side, a few

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:26.610
inches apart, thus introducing interesting
directional bending characteristics.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:32.870
For instance, this one shows a strong collapsing
behavior, and can grip our finger at the same

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.880
time.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.400
Example number three.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.750
Bendy plates.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:45.090
We can even design structures where one side
absorbs deformations, while the other one

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:53.550
transfers it forward, bending the whole structure.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.210
Example number four.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.480
Seat-like structures.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:02.419
The seat surface is designed to deform a little
more to create a comfy sensation, but the

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:10.080
rest of the seat has to be rigid to not collapse
and last a long time.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.060
And finally, example number five.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:14.810
Knee-like structures.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:18.860
These freely collapse in this direction to
allow movement.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:23.610
However, they resist forces from any other
direction.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:29.240
And these are really just some rudimentary
examples of what this method can do, but the

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:35.389
structures showcased here could be used in
soft robotics, soft mechanisms, prosthetics,

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:37.440
and even more areas.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.129
The main challenge of this work is creating
an algorithm that can deal with these breaking

00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:46.940
patterns, which make for an object that is
nearly impossible to manufacture.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:52.060
However, this method can not only eliminate
these, but it can design structures that can

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:58.569
be manufactured on low-end 3D printers and
it also uses inexpensive materials to accomplish

00:05:58.569 --> 00:05:59.729
that.

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:05.050
And hold on to your papers, because this work
showcases a handcrafted technique to perform

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.050
all this.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.379
Not a learning algorithm in sight.

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:13.169
And there are two more things that I really
liked in this paper.

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:18.419
One is that these proposed structures collapse
way better than this previous method.

00:06:18.419 --> 00:06:23.870
And, not only the source code of this project
is available, but it is available for you

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:28.729
to try on one of the best websites on the
entirety of the internet.

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:30.310
Shadertoy.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:31.310
So good!

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:37.730
So, I hope you now agree that the field of
3D printing research is improving at an incredible

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.970
pace, and I also hope that you had some fun
learning about it.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:07:19.729
What a time 

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:31.039
to 

00:07:31.039 --> 00:07:44.530
be alive!

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:48.349
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

